---
title: Trust & the sandbox
---

Installing a plugin should never be a security decision you have to think hard
about. LogIt's plugin model is built around that.

## Restricted Mode

Community plugins that run code are **off by default**. The app ships and works
with only its built-in extension points until you explicitly turn them on, with
a one-time warning. Content packs (exercise packs) are pure data and always
install.

## Install once, verify, run local

On install the app:

1. Fetches the artifact once.
2. Computes its SHA-256 and compares it to `manifest.integrity`
   (`sha256-<base64>`). A mismatch is refused.
3. Stores the artifact locally.

After that it runs offline. Updates are an explicit, reviewable action — a new
`version` and a new hash.

Generate the hash for your artifact:

```bash
printf 'sha256-%s\n' "$(openssl dgst -sha256 -binary bundle.js | openssl base64)"
```

## The sandbox

Code plugins run inside **QuickJS compiled to WebAssembly** — the same on web,
iOS and Android. Inside it a plugin gets a bare ES2020 environment:

- **no DOM**, no `document`, no `window`
- **no `fetch`**, no network of any kind
- **no storage** — `localStorage`, `IndexedDB`, cookies are all absent
- **no timers** — `setTimeout`, `setInterval` don't exist
- a **fresh VM per call** — nothing persists between runs
- a **hard wall-clock deadline** (~300 ms) — an infinite loop is killed

Your plugin receives a frozen JSON input and must return a JSON-serialisable
value. That's the entire interface. State (for progression) is threaded through
the return value — never a module-level variable.

## Why LogIt can do this

Every code-plugin type is a **pure function**: it takes data and returns data.
It doesn't render UI, register commands, or integrate with the app's internals —
so there's nothing it needs the wider app for, and everything can be sandboxed.
Widgets are the same: a widget returns a *description* of what to show, and the
app draws it.

This is a deliberate trade-off, the same one Apple WidgetKit and Android Glance
make: a plugin can only compose the primitives the host ships. In exchange, you
never have to trust plugin code with your training history or your sync
credentials.
